---
title: A tour of MyST
date: 2026-04-15
summary: What this stack can do when Markdown is not enough.
---

Plain Markdown carries an essay until the essay needs a sidenote, a cross-reference,
or a figure with a caption. MyST picks up from there: a superset of CommonMark
with a directive and role system borrowed from reStructuredText.

## Admonitions

:::{note}
Admonitions are directives. The fence syntax uses three colons so the inside
stays plain Markdown — no indentation gymnastics.
:::

:::{warning}
Use them sparingly. A page of yellow boxes reads like a compliance manual.
:::

## Figures with captions

:::{figure} /static/img/minard.png
:alt: Minard's figurative map of Napoleon's 1812 Russian campaign
:align: center

Charles Joseph Minard, *Carte figurative des pertes successives en hommes de
l'Armée Française dans la campagne de Russie 1812–1813*. Six variables on a
single sheet — the canonical argument for information density.
:::

## Footnotes

Tufte's essays carry their apparatus in the margin[^tufte]. MyST keeps them
at the foot of the page, which is closer to how the web wants to render them.

[^tufte]: Edward Tufte, *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information*, 1983.

## Definition lists

Signal
: Something that changes the reader's model of the world.

Noise
: Everything else.

## Tables

| Format   | Directives | Roles | Ecosystem       |
| -------- | :--------: | :---: | --------------- |
| CommonMark |     —     |   —   | universal       |
| MyST     |     ✓      |   ✓   | Sphinx, JupyterBook |
| RST      |     ✓      |   ✓   | Python docs     |

## Math

Inline math like $E = mc^2$ drops into a sentence. Display math stands on its own:

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}
$$

